::ALG2_U05_L05_Q01::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q01
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+1 and g(x)=2x, what is (f+g)(x)?
{
=A. 3x+1#Correct. Add x+1 and 2x.
~B. 2x^2+1#This multiplies.
~C. 3x#This omits 1.
~D. 2x+1#This is f(g(x)).
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q02::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q02
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x-3 and g(x)=4x, what is (f-g)(x)?
{
~A. 5x-3#This adds instead of subtracting.
=B. -3x-3#Correct. Subtract: x-3-4x=-3x-3.
~C. 3x+3#This subtracts in the wrong order.
~D. 4x-3#This is f(g(x)).
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q03::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q03
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x and g(x)=x+2, what is (fg)(x)?
{
~A. 2x+2#This adds the function rules instead of multiplying them.
~B. x+2#This is just g(x).
=C. x^2+2x#Correct. Multiply x(x+2).
~D. x^2+2#This misses the product term.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q04::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q04
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+5 and g(x)=x-1, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. x+6#This uses x+1.
~B. x^2+4x-5#This multiplies the functions.
~C. 2x+4#This adds the functions.
=D. x+4#Correct. Substitute x-1 into x+5.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q05::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q05
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=2x+1 and g(x)=x^2, what is f(g(x))?
{
=A. 2x^2+1#Correct. Substitute x^2 into f.
~B. (2x+1)^2#This is g(f(x)).
~C. 2x+1+x^2#This adds functions.
~D. 2x^3+x^2#This multiplies.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q06::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q06
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x^2 and g(x)=x+3, what is g(f(x))?
{
~A. (x+3)^2#This is f(g(x)).
=B. x^2+3#Correct. Substitute x^2 into g.
~C. x^2(x+3)#This multiplies.
~D. 2x+3#This is not the composition.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q07::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q07
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=3x and g(x)=x-2, what is g(f(4))?
{
~A. 6#This applies g to 4 first.
~B. 12#This stops after f(4).
=C. 10#Correct. f(4)=12, then g(12)=10.
~D. 18#This adds instead of subtracting.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q08::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q08
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+4 and g(x)=2x, what is f(g(5))?
{
~A. 18#This applies f first, then g.
~B. 10#This stops after g(5).
~C. 9#This applies f to 5 only.
=D. 14#Correct. g(5)=10 and f(10)=14.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q09::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q09
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
For (f/g)(x) with g(x)=x-6, what value is excluded?
{
=A. x=6#Correct. The denominator is zero when x=6.
~B. x=-6#This has the wrong sign.
~C. x=0#g(0)=-6, not zero.
~D. No values#A denominator zero must be excluded.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q10::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q10
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
For (f/g)(x) with g(x)=x+3, what value is excluded?
{
~A. x=3#This uses the opposite sign; x+3 equals zero when x=-3.
=B. x=-3#Correct. The denominator is zero when x=-3.
~C. x=0#g(0)=3, so the denominator is not zero at x=0.
~D. All real numbers#Only x=-3 is excluded.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q11::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q11
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
Which notation means composition?
{
~A. f(x)g(x)#This means multiplication.
~B. f(x)+g(x)#This means addition.
=C. f(g(x))#Correct. This means apply g first, then f.
~D. f(x)-g(x)#This means subtraction.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q12::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q12
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x^2 and g(x)=2x, what is f(g(3))?
{
~A. 18#This multiplies 6 by 3.
~B. 12#This adds steps incorrectly.
~C. 9#This applies f to 3 only.
=D. 36#Correct. g(3)=6 and f(6)=36.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q13::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q13
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+7 and g(x)=x/2, what is f(g(10))?
{
=A. 12#Correct. g(10)=5 and f(5)=12.
~B. 8.5#This applies f first and then divides.
~C. 5#This stops after g(10).
~D. 17#This applies f to 10 only.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q14::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q14
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x-1 and g(x)=x+1, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. x+2#This adds functions.
=B. x#Correct. Substitute x+1 into x-1.
~C. x^2-1#This multiplies functions.
~D. x-2#This uses the wrong order.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q15::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q15
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=2x and g(x)=x+8, what is (f+g)(4)?
{
~A. 16#This omits part of the sum.
~B. 12#This is f(4)+g(0) style error.
=C. 20#Correct. (f+g)(x)=3x+8, so at x=4 the value is 20.
~D. 40#This multiplies the outputs.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q16::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q16
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+2 and g(x)=x-2, what is (fg)(3)?
{
~A. 6#This adds the outputs.
~B. 1#This is g(3) only.
~C. 25#This squares f(3).
=D. 5#Correct. f(3)=5 and g(3)=1, so product is 5.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q17::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q17
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
What is the domain issue in dividing functions?
{
=A. The denominator function cannot equal zero.#Correct. Division by zero is undefined.
~B. The numerator cannot equal zero.#A zero numerator is allowed.
~C. The input must be positive only.#That depends on the functions involved.
~D. There is never a restriction.#Denominator zeros create restrictions.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q18::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q18
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x^2 and g(x)=x-4, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. x^2-4#This subtracts after squaring.
=B. (x-4)^2#Correct. Substitute x-4 into f.
~C. x^2(x-4)#This multiplies.
~D. 2x-4#This is not the composition.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q19::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q19
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=x+3 and g(x)=x^2, what is g(f(x))?
{
~A. x^2+3#This is f(g(x)).
~B. x^2+3x#This multiplies incorrectly.
=C. (x+3)^2#Correct. Substitute x+3 into g.
~D. 2x+3#This is not composition.
}


::ALG2_U05_L05_Q20::
Question ID: ALG2_U05_L05_Q20
MLA Standard: MLA.A2.FN.7; MLA.A2.FN.8
Question:
If f(x)=5x and g(x)=x/5, what is f(g(x))?
{
~A. 25x#This multiplies functions.
~B. x/25#This composes in the wrong way.
~C. 10x#This adds outputs.
=D. x#Correct. Substitute x/5 into 5x.
}

